WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
Good morning, John. I suppose, I should still be making these videos for you,

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:04.680
even though you're not making them for me.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.860
It's part of this thing on Tumblr.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.420
I almost replied, but then I figured,

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.020
no,

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:09.480
this is good enough for a whole video.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.880
It's a piece of what appears to be a science quiz and it asks the question:

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.440
"What is the strongest force on Earth?"

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.400
and the student has written

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.380
"Love"

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.180
and the teacher has crossed it out with a big red x.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:21.500
Well I want to know what the greatest force on Earth actually is

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.920
and also what the teacher thinks it is.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.300
And I just really like to overanalyze stuff

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:27.520
so, let's do that now.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.400
First, physicists will tell you that there are in fact, only

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.420
four forces in the universe.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:33.500
There's gravity,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.980
which is the obvious one that we feel all the time

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.360
cause, you know, it's always trying to make us fall over,

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:39.020
which is super annoying,

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.180
unless you're in bed,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.120
in which case I'm jealous.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.880
Gravity, it turns out, is actually the weakest of the forces.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.380
There's actually electromagnetism,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.820
the weak nuclear force

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.400
and the strong nuclear force.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.000
And as the name suggests,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.220
the strong nuclear force

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.120
is the strongest force.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.600
It is 1 x 10^39 times stronger than gravity .

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.440
For people who don't know scientific notation,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.100
watch Crash Course Chemistry.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.380
Also, it's- it's a lot.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.980
So that's the answer, the actual definite answer to the question is:

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.800
it is the strong force.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:06.840
Done? Right?

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.460
No, not done because probably if the student had put that down,

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.420
the teacher still would have put a big red x through it.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.220
From the question above, which mentions igneous and sedimentary rocks,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.060
I can assume that this is a geology quiz

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:16.979
and with that information

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:19.660
I'm going to guess that the teacher is actually trying to ask:

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.940
What thing has the greatest impact on the Earth's geology?

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.700
And the answer that they're looking for is

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.840
water or the hydrological cycle,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.520
which, yes, has a huge impact on the geology of the Earth.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.300
But you can also make other cases;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.460
you could say that life has the greatest impact on the geology of the Earth

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.780
by creating soils

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:35.920
and preventing erosion

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.780
and transpiring moisture into the atmosphere.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.140
But the more interesting line of reasoning for me is if you interpret the question--

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.340
and yes it is open for interpretation,

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.300
obviously, because it is so poorly worded--

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.500
not what has had the greatest impact on the Earth's geology,

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.380
but what is currently having the greatest impact on the Earth's geology

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.119
and in that case,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.900
it is very easy to make the case that the answer to that question

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.080
is humans.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.120
Humans turn mountains inside out,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.080
dry up lakes,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.460
turn deserts into gardens

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:00.990
and forests into deserts.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.600
But there's more to it than that, right?

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.360
Like: what is the force that drives humans?

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.460
What is the reason why there are so many humans on the planet?

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:08.620
Well, my friends,

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:09.820
what is that force?

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.780
The force that keeps us together,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.380
keeps us feeding our children,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.600
keeps us coupling

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.100
and making more.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:17.600
It's love.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:18.720
It's love isn't it?

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.200
It's really- it's sex.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.209
It's... yeah..

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:23.060
But you could make the case

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.880
that the answer to the question the teacher thinks that they are asking,

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.880
(that they're not actually asking) is

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:28.660
love.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:29.220
And that,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.440
my friends, is how to BS your way

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.280
into an A on a geology test.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.200
I do have to say that that's not the actual answer though,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.271
because the actual answer is the energy of the Sun,

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.460
without which the hydrological cycle, trees and people would not exist.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.880
[Intermission music from Monty Python and The Holy Grail]

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.020
John and I were talking recently about how we like to think that we understand this community,

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:48.959
that we, you know, interact with constantly,

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:50.180
but we probably really don't,

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.660
at least, not to like a great degree of accuracy.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:53.729
So John and I decided that it would be

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:56.140
a good idea to do a census of Nerdfighteria.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.980
There's a link in the description to a survey; it's about 30 questions long,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.680
it'll take 10 to 30 minutes,

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.920
and we would really, really appreciate it if you take the time to fill it out.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.880
Craig, thank you for your amazing video this week

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:06.840
and Grace,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:07.980
I will see you on Tuesday.

